### What is flatMapToInt?

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

record Account(String id, int[] dailyAmounts) {}

List<Account> accounts = List.of(
    new Account("SAV2001", new int[]{5000, 3000, 7000}),
    new Account("CUR3001", new int[]{15000, 8000}));

// flatMapToInt: one step, zero boxing
int total = accounts.stream()
    .flatMapToInt(a -> IntStream.of(a.dailyAmounts()))
    .sum();
// 5000 + 3000 + 7000 + 15000 + 8000 = 38000
```

### How does it compare to flatMap+mapToInt?

```java
// Two steps with boxing:
int total = accounts.stream()
    .flatMap(a -> java.util.Arrays.stream(a.dailyAmounts()).boxed())  // Stream<Integer>
    .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)  // unbox back to int
    .sum();
// Same result, but creates Integer objects in between

// One step, no boxing:
int total = accounts.stream()
    .flatMapToInt(a -> IntStream.of(a.dailyAmounts()))
    .sum();
```

### What is the comparison table?

| Approach | Boxing | Steps | Performance |
|----------|:---:|:---:|-------|
| flatMapToInt | None | 1 | Best |
| flatMap + mapToInt | Integer wrappers | 2 | Worse |
| flatMap + boxed | Integer wrappers | 2 | Worst |

### How do flatMapToLong and flatMapToDouble work?

```java
// flatMapToLong: for long primitives
long totalPaise = accounts.stream()
    .flatMapToLong(a -> java.util.stream.LongStream.of(a.dailyAmountsPaise()))
    .sum();

// flatMapToDouble: for double primitives
double totalRate = loans.stream()
    .flatMapToDouble(l -> java.util.stream.DoubleStream.of(l.monthlyRates()))
    .average()
    .orElse(0.0);
```

### What are real banking use cases?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class FlatMapToIntDemo {
    record Branch(String name, int[] dailyTransactionCounts) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Branch> branches = List.of(
            new Branch("Mumbai", new int[]{150, 200, 175, 190, 210}),
            new Branch("Delhi", new int[]{120, 135, 140, 155, 160}));

        // Total transactions across all branches
        int totalTxns = branches.stream()
            .flatMapToInt(b -> IntStream.of(b.dailyTransactionCounts()))
            .sum();
        System.out.println("Total transactions: " + totalTxns);

        // Max single-day transactions
        int maxDay = branches.stream()
            .flatMapToInt(b -> IntStream.of(b.dailyTransactionCounts()))
            .max()
            .orElse(0);
        System.out.println("Peak day: " + maxDay);

        // Summary statistics
        var stats = branches.stream()
            .flatMapToInt(b -> IntStream.of(b.dailyTransactionCounts()))
            .summaryStatistics();
        System.out.println("Stats: " + stats);
    }
}
```

```text
Total transactions: 1635
Peak day: 210
Stats: IntSummaryStatistics{count=10, sum=1635, min=120, average=163.5, max=210}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: unnecessary boxing with flatMap + boxed
int total = accounts.stream()
    .flatMap(a -> java.util.Arrays.stream(a.dailyAmounts()).boxed()) // boxing!
    .mapToInt(i -> i) // unboxing!
    .sum();

// CORRECT: flatMapToInt avoids boxing entirely
int total = accounts.stream()
    .flatMapToInt(a -> IntStream.of(a.dailyAmounts()))
    .sum();
```

### When should you prefer flatMap over flatMapToInt?

Use flatMap when the intermediate stream needs non-primitive operations like filter with object Predicates or collect into a List.

```java
// flatMap needed: collecting to List<Integer>
List<Integer> allAmounts = accounts.stream()
    .flatMap(a -> java.util.Arrays.stream(a.dailyAmounts()).boxed())
    .toList();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- flatMapToInt is the idiomatic choice when flattening to primitives and then aggregating.
- For small datasets the boxing overhead is negligible; for large datasets it matters (GC pressure).
- IntStream.of(int[]) and Arrays.stream(int[]) both produce IntStream from arrays.
- flatMapToInt works with parallel streams the same way as flatMap.
- There is no flatMapToObj. flatMap already returns an object stream.
